{"from": "text:res-gestae-divi-augusti-8", "type": "attests", "to": "person:caesar-augustus", "grade": "A", "note": "Augustus's own funerary inscription names him conducting the census/lustrum three times (28 BCE with Agrippa, 8 BCE alone, 14 CE with Tiberius): the emperor of Luke 2:1 issuing census decrees."}
{"from": "text:res-gestae-divi-augusti-8", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-02-01-augustus-decree", "grade": "C", "note": "The Res Gestae documents Augustus decreeing and conducting censuses - the same ruler's act of registration. The censuses it records are lustra of Roman citizens, not an empire-wide enrollment; 'all the world' is Luke's phrase, and no single empire-wide census under Augustus is attested (see the claim's documented source). Regraded B to C by architect review: the Res Gestae records censuses of Roman citizens, a different institution from a provincial enrollment; it is context for a census-taking emperor."}
{"from": "artifact:quirinius-syria-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "person:quirinius", "grade": "B", "note": "Coins issued by Quirinius's administration in Syria, inscribed with 'the 36th year of Caesar [Augustus]' (5/6 CE), name his governorship of Syria. Regraded A to B by architect review: the fetched source says the coins were issued by his administration and dated to 5/6 CE, not that they name him (the Pilate-coin precedent)."}
{"from": "artifact:titulus-venetus", "type": "attests", "to": "person:quirinius", "grade": "A", "note": "The stele of Quintus Aemilius Secundus, an officer who served under Quirinius: 'By order of the same Quirinius I took a census of the city of Apamea' - Quirinius directing a census in Roman Syria."}
{"from": "artifact:titulus-venetus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-02-02-quirinius-governor", "grade": "B", "note": "A census of Apamea (Syria) taken by order of Quirinius: the same person and the same event-type (a Syrian enrollment) that Luke 2:2 names. The stele is generally tied to the 6 CE legateship; the Wikipedia Quirinius article calls it validation of the census he conducted in Syria. Regraded A to B by reviewer 6: the stele names Quirinius and a census of Apamea in Syria but carries no office title, so under 'A grade covers the whole claim' (the Caiaphas-ossuary precedent: the ossuary went A to B for the office because it bears no title) it is B for the governorship of Luke 2:2; it stays A for person:quirinius."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-1-taxation", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-02-02-quirinius-governor", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 18.1: Cyrenius (Quirinius) came into Judea, which was added to the province of Syria, to take an inventory of their substance - Quirinius conducting the enrollment as governor of Syria in 6 CE. His date is a decade after Herod's death (see the complicates edges on the timing claim)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-1-circumcision", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-02-21-circumcision-eighth-day", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus records the ancestral charge that sons 'should be circumcised in the flesh of their foreskin... on the eighth day after they were born' - the same custom in the same region. He attests the custom, not this particular circumcision."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-keritot-1-7", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-02-22-24-purification-turtledoves", "grade": "B", "note": "The Mishnah's dove-price incident is expressly about the offerings 'of a woman after childbirth' - the same purification offering of bird-pairs in Jerusalem that Luke 2:24 names ('a pair of turtledoves, or two young pigeons'). Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe 1st-century practice exactly."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-6-422-425", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-02-41-annual-passover", "grade": "C", "note": "Cestius's Passover count: 256,500 lambs and about 2,700,000 worshippers at the festival - Jerusalem's Passover pilgrimage on a scale that makes an annual family pilgrimage plausible. Crowd sizes are context (C), not evidence of this family's custom."}
{"from": "artifact:caesarea-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-02-39-return-nazareth", "grade": "C", "note": "The inscription names Nazareth as a village of priestly settlement - the village existed, though the stone is late 3rd or early 4th century, centuries after the event it would frame."}
{"from": "site:nazareth-house", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-02-39-return-nazareth", "grade": "B", "note": "A first-century dwelling excavated at Nazareth confirms an inhabited village there in the early Roman period - the destination the family returned to. It attests the setting, not the family's return itself."}
{"from": "observation:lunar-eclipse-4-bce-03-13", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:lk-02-02-enrollment-timing", "note": "The partial lunar eclipse of 13 March 4 BCE anchors Josephus's chronology of Herod's death in 4 BCE. Quirinius's census falls in 6 CE (Josephus Ant. 18.1-2; Cassius Dio on Archelaus's exile) - roughly a decade after Herod's death - so an enrollment 'when Quirinius was governor of Syria' cannot also fall in the days of Herod as Luke 1:5 implies."}
{"from": "observation:lunar-eclipse-1-bce-01-10", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:lk-02-02-enrollment-timing", "note": "Even on the alternative 1 BCE eclipse chronology (Steinmann), Herod's death still precedes Quirinius's 6 CE census by about seven years, so the tension between Luke 2:2 and Herod's era remains."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-1-taxation", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:lk-02-02-enrollment-timing", "note": "Josephus dates Quirinius's census to 6 CE, after Archelaus's banishment, 37 years after Actium; Luke ties the same enrollment to the birth of Jesus. Most critical scholars judge Luke in error here (Wikipedia, Census of Quirinius); defenses invoke unproven earlier censuses or a disputed double governorship."}
{"from": "artifact:lapis-tiburtinus", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:lk-02-02-quirinius-governor", "note": "The Lapis Tiburtinus, invoked to give Quirinius an earlier Syrian command, is attributed by most scholars to a Piso; a minority theory posits Quirinius, but the fetched List of Roman governors of Syria footnote notes there is no evidence he was ever Proconsul of Asia. Quirinius's documented Syrian governorship is 6-12 CE (Wikipedia, List of Roman governors of Syria: '6 - 12 Publius Sulpicius Quirinius')."}
