{"from": "claim:lk-02-01-augustus-decree", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-census-of-quirinius-no-empire-census", "note": "Modern scholarship on the decree's scope: no single empire-wide census under Augustus, client kingdoms not directly taxed."}
{"from": "claim:lk-02-02-quirinius-governor", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-quirinius", "note": "Quirinius's documented legateship of Syria begins in 6 CE; the governors list gives 6-12 CE with Saturninus (9-7/6 BCE) and Varus (7/6-4 BCE) before him."}
{"from": "claim:lk-02-02-enrollment-timing", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-census-of-quirinius-luke-error", "note": "Modern scholarship: Luke cites Herod's era and the census of Quirinius together; most critical scholars agree Luke is in error."}
{"from": "claim:lk-02-03-own-city", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-census-of-quirinius-no-empire-census", "note": "Modern scholarship: no Roman census required travel from one's own home to ancestral homes, against the practice Luke 2:3 describes. The prefect Gaius Vibius Maximus's 104 CE edict ordering Egyptians to 'return to their own homes' for the registration could not be fetched this run (no Wikipedia article; Select Papyri not on Wikisource); recorded under Gaps in notes.md."}
{"from": "claim:lk-02-07-manger", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-luke-2", "note": "Modern scholarship on the wording: kataluma ('the inn') may mean 'guest room'; phatne is a feeding trough. No ancient locus for the manger detail was found this run."}
{"from": "claim:lk-02-46-temple-teachers", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-luke-2", "note": "The fetched Luke 2 article describes the scene (Jesus found 'in the temple talking with the temple teachers'); no independent ancient evidence for teachers sitting in the temple courts was found this run."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-annals-3-48", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-annals-3"}
{"from": "text:tacitus-annals-3-48", "type": "attests", "to": "person:quirinius", "grade": "A", "note": "Tacitus records Tiberius's request that 'the death of Sulpicius Quirinus be celebrated with a public funeral' and his career: consul under Augustus, triumph for the Homonadenses in Cilicia, adviser to Caius Caesar in Armenia. The same Roman aristocrat; Tacitus does not mention the Syrian governorship."}
{"from": "artifact:bethlehem-bulla", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-02-04-journey-bethlehem", "grade": "C", "note": "The bulla ('From the town of Bethlehem to the King', 8th-7th century BCE) confirms Bethlehem as a real Judean administrative town centuries before the journey; it attests the destination's existence, not the journey itself. Reused from Matthew 2."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-annals-3-48", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-annals-3-career"}
